\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ezekiel 17 starts with a riddle, a parable. It says as much - and in saying as much, it gives a hint that the rhetorical force of this chapter will partially come not, as in Ezekiel 16, from openly shocking recasting of common narrative, but from being at least temporarily nonobvious about where the story is going. \
The audience may be able to work out what the riddle means - though the Bible definitely contains examples of riddles which the audience could not work out unless given an interpretation, and that may be the case here. Either way, the space of possibilities, and thinking them through, can engage an audience, or even get around defenses against hearing the truth. Asking the audience to answer rhetorical questions can make this push for truth, despite defenses, even stronger. The audience must answer the question, not yet knowing who the figures in the parable are. People often change their answer based on who benefits, and the like. Giving their answer before they know who benefits can drive home the logic of the situation which might otherwise be resisted.\
The strategy of doubling, or repeating a second time with variation, may also have somewhat of this effect. At the very least, it should prepare us for elements earlier in the oracle to come back, perhaps from a second angle or with a second purpose or meaning.\
Moreover, as in Ezekiel 16 and many previous chapters, the nature of who knows what, when, is key to the rhetorical strategy. The LORD is sending this oracle by means of his prophet, Ezekiel. That means the LORD can alter future understanding of events by means of present prophecy. Near future events, when they happen in accordance with present prediction and interpretation, can be very persuasive to even a rebellious people. As in chapter 16, accusations which might be made against the LORD can be answered in advance. Moreover, far future events, given present interpretation, can change how the people see the present and also the near future when that comes to pass.\
This is, of course, hardly an exhaustive list of the rhetorical strategies Ezekiel 17 might be employing. But it should give us a start.\
With that introduction, let's walk through what Ezekiel is doing, with a preamble, then a riddle and parable, then rhetorical questions, then an interpretation. Who knows what, when? What might the audience think, at various points? That is to say, with each verse, and with each section, what possibilities might be in the mind of the audience? While the oracle as a whole clearly has an interpretation, the possibilities the audience may be considering are as much part of the rhetorical strategy as the interpretation. Then, what is the impact of the shifts in what the audience might be thinking, verse by verse, section by section? What, given all the evidence - with a focus on the exact text - and whatever we can determine of what Ezekiel's rhetorical strategies might be, is Ezekiel doing with each particular verse, as an individual contribution and cumulatively up to that point?
