\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Looking at Ezekiel 17's opening moves, the rhetorical architecture becomes clear through tracking what the audience can and cannot know at each point.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Verses 1-2: The Setup
\b0  "Son of man, riddle a riddle and parable a parable to the house of Israel."\
The audience knows they're about to receive coded communication. This creates interpretive alertness - they must decode, not just receive. The doubling (
\f2 \uc0\u1495 \u1460 \u1497 \u1491 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 /
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1464 \u1513 \u1464 \u1473 \u1500 
\f1 ) suggests multiple layers of meaning will operate simultaneously. They're being asked to think, not just listen.\

\b Verses 3-6: First Eagle and Vine
\b0  The great eagle with multicolored plumage comes to Lebanon, takes the cedar's crown, carries it to a merchant land. Plants seed that becomes a low, spreading vine whose branches turn toward the eagle while roots remain underneath.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The audience would immediately think of Babylon as the great eagle - the imagery of great wings and coming to Lebanon points that direction. The cedar of Lebanon traditionally represents royalty/nobility. But who is the vine? The description of "low spreading vine" (
\f2 \uc0\u1490 \u1462 \u1508 \u1462 \u1503 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1505 \u1465 \u1512 \u1463 \u1495 \u1463 \u1514 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1513 \u1460 \u1473 \u1508 \u1456 \u1500 \u1463 \u1514 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1511 \u1493 \u1465 \u1502 \u1464 \u1492 
\f1 ) suggests deliberate subjugation. </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 At this point, the audience likely suspects: Eagle = Babylon, Cedar top = Jehoiachin's deportation (597 BCE), Seed planted = Zedekiah's installation. The vine turning its branches toward the eagle while roots stay put suggests vassal relationship - appearing loyal while maintaining local base.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\b \cf2 Verses 7-8: Second Eagle
\b0  "And there was another great eagle" - the vine bends its roots toward this one, sends branches to it for watering, despite being planted in good soil with abundant water.\
The audience now faces ambiguity. Second eagle = Egypt seems likely (the only other regional power), but why does the vine redirect toward it? They're processing a story of political realignment, but the moral valence remains unclear. Is the vine being resourceful or faithless?\

\b Verses 9-10: Rhetorical Questions
\b0  "Say: Thus says Lord YHWH: Will it prosper? Will he not pull up its roots and strip its fruit?"\
Here Ezekiel forces the audience to judge before knowing full details. The rhetorical questions demand evaluation: Can a vine that redirects its dependency succeed? The answer seems obvious - no. But they haven't been told explicitly that this is about Zedekiah's rebellion. They must commit to the principle before the application.\
The east wind reference (
\f2 \uc0\u1512 \u1493 \u1468 \u1495 \u1463 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1492 \u1463 \u1511 \u1464 \u1468 \u1491 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1 ) would trigger memory of judgment - it's the destroying wind from the desert. The repeated emphasis on withering (
\f2 \uc0\u1514 \u1460 \u1468 \u1497 \u1489 \u1463 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1497 \u1464 \u1489 \u1465 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1 ) creates certainty about outcome.\

\b Verses 11-21: The Interpretation
\b0  Now Ezekiel makes it explicit: Babylon took Jerusalem's king and nobles, made a covenant with the seed of royalty (Zedekiah), kept it low to ensure dependence. But he rebelled, sending to Egypt for military support.\
The critical shift: In verse 19, YHWH claims the violated oath as "MY oath" (
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1464 \u1500 \u1464 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 ), the broken covenant as "MY covenant" (
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1468 \u1489 \u1456 \u1512 \u1460 \u1497 \u1514 \u1460 \u1497 
\f1 ). What seemed like political maneuvering between nations becomes theological betrayal. The audience must recalibrate - this isn't just about Realpolitik but about sacral oaths taken in YHWH's name.\

\b Verses 22-24: The Reversal
\b0  YHWH himself becomes the planter, taking from the cedar's crown, planting on Israel's high mountain. This becomes a noble cedar where every bird dwells.\
The rhetorical impact: After establishing that human political maneuvering (switching dependencies between eagles) fails, YHWH demonstrates what successful planting looks like. The contrast is total - human planting creates low vines that wither; divine planting creates towering cedars that shelter all.\

\b Overall Rhetorical Strategy:
\b0 \
The parable works through progressive disclosure and forced commitment. The audience must:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	1.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Judge the vine's behavior before knowing it represents their king\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	2.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Acknowledge the obvious futility of the vine's strategy\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	3.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Discover their king is that vine\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	4.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Learn that political oaths invoke divine accountability\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	5.	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 See YHWH's alternative future that bypasses human political schemes\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The doubling intensifies this: First as riddle (what does this mean?), then as interpretation (this is Zedekiah), then as theological claim (oaths to Babylon are oaths before Me), finally as eschatological promise (I will plant differently).\
What makes this particularly powerful: Zedekiah likely justified his Egyptian alliance as resistance to oppression, seeking freedom. But the parable reveals it as violating sacred oaths. The audience cannot claim ignorance - they're being told in advance that this rebellion constitutes theological betrayal, not political liberation.\
This connects to our broader question about accountability: Babylon and Egypt function here as moral agents whose actions create theological consequences. They're not just political entities but participants in divine sovereignty. The covenant between Babylon and Judah, even under coercion, carries divine weight because oaths invoke YHWH's name.
