\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 While eagles and cedars are royal imagery, vines could stand for a person in general as in wisdom literature, or for a social class, or for the nation as a whole. They might have considered other possibilities still. Yes, we know now what was intended. But did the audience, when the vine was first introduced? They don't have the interpretation at that point.\
Similarly, even if the vine is Zedekiah, who is the first eagle? The LORD? Babylon? If - because the audience doesn't know yet, and so much consider other possibilities - the vine is not Zedekiah, what else might the eagles be? What might be the overall thrust of the parable for the other possibilities?\
What might they make of the way the first eagle is greatest, fullest, in its features, but the second is only great, full? Once they have been helped to see the Babylon/Egypt interpretation? Under other interpretations?\
What might they think of the actions of the first eagle, and the inaction of the second eagle? Once they have been helped to see the Babylon/Egypt interpretation? Under other interpretations?\
What might they think of the east wind if they associate it with that phrase as part of the Exodus event? Once they have been helped to see the Babylon/Egypt interpretation? Under other interpretations?\
Looking at everything you've added to what you had earlier seen, how does that impact the overall rhetorical structure?\
How does the oath violation operate then, in the larger rhetorical structure?
