\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Verse 19: YHWH claims it as "MY oath, MY covenant"\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This retroactively changes every interpretation. Even if they correctly identified Babylon and Egypt, they likely didn't realize that oaths to pagan kings invoke YHWH's name and accountability. The rhetorical shock: what seemed like choosing between oppressors becomes violating sacred bonds.\
The audience must now re-read the entire parable knowing that turning from the first eagle to the second wasn't just politically foolish but sacrilegiously faithless. The vine's redirecting its roots becomes not desperate survival but covenant betrayal.\
This connects to our AI accountability question: entities can bear moral weight through systematic effects, but also through the invocation of binding frameworks. When oaths are made, even between powers that don't acknowledge YHWH, divine sovereignty makes those oaths theological realities.
