\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 What Zedekiah in fact did, when seen through the lens of this riddle, then, is rather different from what he intended. Even more so, the results were different from what he intended - though on the matter of the oath specifically it should be noted that 2 Chronicles 36:13, while noting that because he was rebellious he did not turn to the LORD for help, does tell us that Zedekiah had been forced to swear the oath to Nebuchadnezzar. But that same verse tells us that the oath had been to God. That the oath was forced is backed up by the grammar of verse 13, which may be read 'putting him under oath'. Similarly, the part of the parable for which that is an interpretation is active: planted it. But while the text acknowledges this, the focus is on the contempt with which Zedekiah treated the oath, verse 18, and whose oath it counts as - 'surely it is my oath that he has treated with contempt', verse 19. Because of this, the one acting in response is the LORD: "I will".\
That it was an oath to the LORD does seem to be relevant in applying this outside the case of Zedekiah. While there may be some warning here against breaking oaths in general, this specific case is a question of having broken an oath to the LORD. So we must be properly cautious in generalizing.\
Similarly, the covenant which Zedekiah broke, the LORD's covenant, makes generalizing this case much less straightforward.\
But that does not mean we cannot learn anything from this case. Far from it. \
For one, we have what the parable tells us about the difference between what Zedekiah intended and what he was in fact doing, and the difference between what resulted and what he thought would happen.\
For another, the dual framework, while it must be used cautiously, gives some hints about the nature of apparently secular power, realist considerations, consequential reasoning, and even truth itself.\
And finally, while this oath is to God, the wording 'he despised the oath' comes before the discussion of who the oath was really to. Treating oaths with contempt, even treating truth with contempt, is unwise at best, and inviting God's judgement at worst.
