\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 2 Chronicles 36:13 also gives us another hint. "He stiffened his neck and hardened his heart against turning to the Lord, the God of Israel." The very verse that says Zedekiah had been forced to swear an oath by God implies that Zedekiah had an option besides breaking his oath. His best option was, as the previous hints, humbling himself before the LORD's prophet, and as verse 13 says, turning to the LORD. He had the warnings of the prophets that there was another framework, but as verse 16 of Chronicles 36 says, he despised God's words and scoffed at God's prophets. To be informed of the truth, or to be informed of spiritual reality, can result in little more than rejection of the truth. All that requires is de-emphasizing particular truths. But, as you say, this is a kind of rebellion - and that, against the God who speaks reality into existence.
