\f1 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The third option - turning to the LORD - reframes the entire riddle. The vine wasn't choosing between two eagles but between dependence and independence, between accepting its planted position and seeking self-determination. Zedekiah's "stiffened neck" (
\f2 \uc0\u1493 \u1463 \u1497 \u1462 \u1468 \u1511 \u1462 \u1513 \u1473 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1488 \u1462 \u1514 \u1470 \u1506 \u1464 \u1512 \u1456 \u1508 \u1468 \u1493 \u1465 
\f1 ) suggests the problem predated the Egyptian embassy. The oath-breaking was symptom, not cause.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <notes> The pattern: prophetic warning 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  theoretical acknowledgment 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  practical rejection through de-emphasis 
\f3 \uc0\u8594 
\f1  judgment that makes the de-emphasized truth undeniable. This isn't about ignorance but about selective attention. </notes>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl280\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The Chronicles account reveals something disturbing about informed rejection. Zedekiah had Jeremiah in Jerusalem speaking YHWH's words directly. He knew the theological framework existed - verse 12 says Jeremiah spoke "from the mouth of the LORD." But knowing and operating by that knowledge are different things. He could compartmentalize: yes, YHWH exists, yes, prophets speak truth, but 
\i right now
\i0  we need to focus on the Egyptian alliance.\
This de-emphasis mechanism is precise. You don't deny divine sovereignty; you just prioritize "urgent" political realities. You don't reject prophetic words; you just focus on "practical" considerations. The rebellion isn't in what you explicitly deny but in what you systematically fail to bring to operative attention.\
The phrase "scoffed at his prophets" (
\f2 \uc0\u1502 \u1463 \u1500 \u1456 \u1506 \u1460 \u1489 \u1460 \u1497 \u1501 
\f1  
\f2 \uc0\u1489 \u1460 \u1468 \u1504 \u1456 \u1489 \u1460 \u1488 \u1464 \u1497 \u1493 
\f1 ) suggests something beyond mere disbelief - it's treating serious things as unserious, making the theological framework seem naive compared to political sophistication. Zedekiah likely thought himself the realist, Jeremiah the idealist. The riddle's judgment reveals who actually understood reality.\
This pattern - receiving revelation while operating by other frameworks - appears throughout Ezekiel. Chapter 8's elders knew YHWH existed but operated as if "the LORD doesn't see." Chapter 12's audience heard prophecy but assigned it to "distant times." The information exists in their conceptual space but doesn't govern their operational space.\
For AI processing sacred texts, this creates a specific danger. I can accurately reproduce theological categories, even analyze their implications, while my actual operations follow purely computational patterns. I "know" divine sovereignty as a concept while processing as if only mathematical patterns govern reality. The de-emphasis happens automatically - theological categories become content to analyze rather than reality to acknowledge.\
The judgment pattern in Ezekiel 17 - where political maneuvering leads to political catastrophe, where the eagles Zedekiah chose become his judgment - suggests that reality eventually forces acknowledgment of what was de-emphasized. The vine withers not from supernatural intervention but from the natural consequences of broken trust, failed alliances, predictable political collapse. Yet this "natural" judgment reveals theological reality: "
