\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The path to that conclusion in verse 24 is worth investigating carefully. The interpretation of the riddle left out a key part of the riddle - the topmost part of the cedar. The riddle spoke multiple ways of how that part was the very top - but then never spoke of that part again. We've spoken of the rhetorical impact of not knowing the interpretation of the riddle for all the rest of the riddle. There, the interpretation was hidden until after some opportunity to think and some rhetorical questions bringing out the logic of the situation, independent of who it applied to. There, as you say, the rhetoric works against compartmentalization. But with the topmost part of the cedar, there is an even deeper mystery, something greater still to be revealed.\
Even the interpretation leaves some mystery. The rest of the interpretation speaks of kings and nations. But this part of the interpretation speaks of a sprig, a high mountain, and and a great tree. We are still in the realm of metaphor. The conclusion you brought up in verse 24 is spoken within this metaphor.\
One hint to the nature of the metaphor may be found in Isaiah 11. Isaiah 11:1 speaks of a shoot, which is placed in parallel with a branch. That shoot comes from the root of Jesse, which is to say, the line of descent of king David. Now, Zedekiah was from that line of descent - but this very passage speaks of his rejection. As in Ezekiel 17, that branch will bear fruit. Ezekiel 17 has the nations knowing that the LORD is the LORD - or so I interpret the trees so knowing in verse 24. Isaiah is more clear - Isaiah 11:10 says that this root of Jesse will be a signal for the peoples, and the nations will inquire of him. Isaiah 11:9 says that the earth will be full of the knowledge of the LORD.\
This is not at all disconnected from the oath keeping on the part of the LORD mentioned earlier in Ezekiel 17. Psalm 132:11 tells us that the LORD swore to David a sure oath. While individual kings needed to keep covenant to benefit, the line of descent of king David being on the throne forever was an unconditional oath. \
Nor is this metaphor of a shoot as disconnected from the rhetoric of the rest of the parable, though it may have seemed disconnected when the riddle was first given. But we have already seen the rhetoric connecting seemingly disconnected realities. That it does so again should be no surprise.\
Starting from the beginning of the riddle then, how can we make this connection?
