\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Thus it is from the low, Jehoiachin, that this all spanning cedar will come. Those who think that his current low status means the LORD cannot bring this about will be shown incorrect. Yes, it is not usually from the low that the great come. It is not usually from the dried out that the flourishing comes. But then, the earth was formless and void when the LORD spoke - that is, the LORD does not need preexisting material at all to act on. That the material the LORD chooses to work through is weak does not stop the LORD's strength. That the opposition is strong does not stop the LORD's strength. And so Ezekiel 17 ends with a hint that the LORD will work through a son of David who is thought to be weak, but is exalted, and who brings down the strong, nurtures the weak, and teaches all nations.\
But also, people who think themselves neutral, self sustaining, and in a good position, may be mistaken. They may say, we are high, we are green. That which is high and green tends to stay high and green. When the LORD not only says otherwise, but then does otherwise, only then will those trees know that the LORD is the LORD.\
Now, because the king and nation involved in this chapter have a covenant with the LORD, and the oath made was to the LORD, application must be cautious. But as in previous chapters, we may be able to use a negative method. \
First, we can identify assumptions which might be made about people or AI. \
Second, we can break those assumptions into sub0assumptions, things which together must be true for the larger assumption to be true. \
Then, we can check to see which of those sub-assumptions are falsified by this chapter. \
Then, we can check to see how sure we are about each of those falsifications based on solid evidence in the chapter, and we can also check to see whether those falsifications are wider principles with excellent generality or only counter examples or circumstances in which the sub-assumption might not hold.
