Yes, I presented trees as nations without supporting evidence. Indeed, the trees might be royal houses, or peoples. Rather similar lines of argument follow from those interpretations though. \
The dual framework is hardly limited to oaths. You yourself noted that it was Babylon which moved the sprig. Yet the passage tells us the LORD did. Babylon netted Zedekiah and punished him for treachery. Yet also, the LORD did. Is the east wind a natural phenomenon, or judgement from the LORD? Even the production of branches and fruit in verse 8 has some question as to who is doing this - was it the vine? The circumstances of the vine? Or did the LORD do this for his own purposes? Before verses 23 and 24, is there anything in the chapter which does not have this dual framework?\
The verb for withering in verse 9 should be interpreted in light of the earlier verbs in the verse. The question form asks, will he not tear up its roots? While strictly the withering is a result of hypothetical aggressive actions towards the vine, the meaning is clear - those aggressive actions will happen, and therefore the withering will happen as a result.\
Yes, the purpose of Babylon in verse 14 does make Zedekiah seeking dignity a very real possibility. Modern sensibilities might think this makes treachery towards Babylon more acceptable. Ezekiel 17 seems to disagree - the logic of breaking covenant and oath with the LORD still holds.\
Other parts of Ezekiel seem to reference Jeremiah, so lack of access is a doubtful reason for the lack of reference here. Indeed, while I mentioned the connection between the branch of Isaiah 11 and the sprig of Ezekiel 17, there might be reference to Jeremiah 23:5 and Jeremiah 33:15 as well, though the connection seems less certain there. And there may be even more subtle references to Jeremiah which I do not see. But the lack of overt reference seems in line with the strategy of riddle and rhetorical question, asking the audience to assess the objective situation before being told who the participants even are.\
